{red|**Imagine yourself genuinely restored, not just surviving but thriving.**

What would that look like for you?}{red| How would you feel?}{red| What would be different about your daily life?}{red| What would be healed that currently hurts?}

Describe what restoration would look like for you:

{red|**Based on your assessment, identify your restoration priorities.**

Which two or three areas most need attention?}

{red|For each priority area, what would healing look like?}

{red|What resources or support might help in each area?}

Write your restoration priorities:

Priority 1: Priority 2: Priority 3:

**This becomes your roadmap for the healing work ahead.**

{red|**Reflect on where you are in emotional healing.**

What signs of emotional healing do you notice in yourself?}{red| What emotional struggles remain?}{red| What has helped your emotional healing so far?}{red| What do you need to continue healing emotionally?}

Write about your emotional healing progress:

{red|**Honestly assess your spiritual state since the divorce.**

How has your relationship with God been affected?}{red| What struggles or barriers exist in your faith?}{red| What spiritual practices have you maintained or abandoned?}{red| What do you need from God right now?}

Write about your spiritual state:

{red|**Assess the practical structures of your life.**

What routines are working well?}{red| What areas feel chaotic or unstable?}{red| What would help you feel more grounded practically?}{red| What new normal do you want to create?}

Write about your practical life assessment:

{red|**Assess your current relational world.**

Which friendships have remained supportive?}{red| Which relationships have you lost or do you need to let go?}{red| Where do you experience community and belonging?}{red| What is your capacity for connection right now?}{red| What do you need relationally for healing?}

Write about your relational assessment:

{red|**Reflect on any growth you have already experienced or can imagine experiencing through this painful season.**

What have you learned about yourself?}{red| How have you grown or changed?}{red| What do you appreciate more now?}{red| What strengths have you discovered?}{red| How might this experience ultimately benefit you or others?}

Write about growth you have experienced or hope for:

{red|**Examine how you talk to yourself about your divorce.**

What does your inner critic say to you?}{red| How harsh or compassionate is your self-talk?}{red| What would you say to a friend in your situation?}{red| What would change if you treated yourself with that same kindness?}

Write about your self-talk patterns:

{red|**Divorce often destroys dreams.** The future you imagined is not going to happen.

**But you can dream again.** Not the same dreams, but new ones appropriate to your new reality.

**Rebuilding dreams starts with allowing yourself to want things again.** What do you hope for?}{red| What would you like your life to look like?}{red| What would bring meaning and joy?}

{red|**These dreams do not have to be fully formed or realistic yet.** You are just beginning to look forward again. Give yourself permission to hope, to want, to imagine.

What do you dream might be possible for your future?}

{red|**Begin to articulate what you hope for in your future.**

What would you like your life to look like in one year?}{red| In five years?}{red| What relationships do you hope for?}{red| What experiences do you hope to have?}{red| What kind of person do you hope to become?}{red| What would make life feel meaningful and good?}

Write about your future hopes:

{red|What is the most important thing you have learned or realized through this journey about restoration after divorce?}

{red|What do you most want to remember as you continue healing?}

Write your key insight:

{red|Based on what you have learned, what commitments will you make to ongoing restoration?}

{red|How will you continue pursuing emotional health?}{red| How will you nurture your spiritual life?}{red| How will you maintain practical stability?}{red| How will you invest in relationships?}{red| How will you practice self-compassion and hope?}

